# A suspended Fabry-Perot cavity held by a servo on the anti-spring side of
# resonance: optics, a suspension and electronics in one model.
#
# The end mirror is tuned to the half-power point where the optical spring is
# negative. On its own the suspension is then unstable: the light pushes the
# mirror further from resonance faster than the pendulum pulls it back. The
# transmitted power is a linear error signal there, and a lead filter feeds it
# back as a force on the mirror, with a unity-gain frequency near 100 Hz.
#
#     l1 ── m1 ══ sc ══ m2 ── trans (readout)
#                       │ sus (pendulum)
#            act ←── servo ←── sense
#
# This file is the single source of truth. Finesse.jl solves the closed-loop
# frequency response; OpticalModelingToolkit.jl simulates the same loop in time,
# including lock acquisition, which a frequency-domain code cannot do.

laser l1 P=1
mirror m1 R=0.99 T=0.01 L=0
mirror m2 R=0.999 T=0.001 L=0 phi=-0.158292
space s0 l1.p1 m1.p1 L=1
space sc m1.p2 m2.p2 L=0.1
pendulum sus m2.mech mass=0.1 fz=20 Qz=2

readout_dc trans m2.p1.o
zpk servo [-188.49556] [-6283.1853] gain=-3.3e-5
wire sense trans.DC.o servo.p1.i
wire act servo.p2.o m2.mech.F_z
